Psalm 116 is a psalm of thanksgiving for deliverance from mortal danger. The psalmist declares their love for the moral order because it has heard their voice and supplication. The psalm describes the psalmist's distress, being surrounded by the snares of death and anguish, and crying out to the moral order for deliverance. The psalmist vows to walk before the moral order in the land of the living, to lift up the cup of salvation, and to pay vows to the moral order in the presence of all the people.

